<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The deceased Ganesan's daughter was married to the accused's son, who had died about eight months before the occurrence after a stray dog bite. The prosecution case was that the accused blamed the death on sorcery by Ganesan, pressed the widowed daughter-in-law to transfer house title and hand over Rs 30,000 from livestock sales, and finally waylaid Ganesan at about 1:15 a.m. on 10 February 2019 as he stepped out, stabbing him with a knife. Ganesan died the next day; the charge was altered from Section 307 to Section 302 IPC. The trial court convicted in June 2023 — life imprisonment under Section 302, with shorter terms under Sections 341 and 506(ii).</p>

<h2>The evidence that carried the case</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Two eyewitnesses</strong><br>The deceased's daughters, found intrinsically reliable despite relationship; they were present, intervened, and were threatened.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Medical corroboration</strong><br>The accident register and post-mortem documented stab and cut injuries consistent with the ocular account.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Prompt FIR</strong><br>Registered in time and before the Magistrate within 24 hours, foreclosing the embellishment argument.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Recovery</strong><br>The knife recovered pursuant to the accused's confession statement.</div>
</div>

<h2>Private defence: the doctrine restated</h2>
<p>The Bench quoted the principle that the right is one "of defence, not of retribution, expected to repel unlawful aggression and not as retaliatory measure". Its application was arithmetical: multiple injuries on the deceased, none on the accused, at a time and place chosen by the accused. No material suggested the deceased was armed and aggressing in fact; the claim that he was waiting armed in the open at 1 a.m. found no support. Without a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt, Section 100 IPC never engaged, and Sections 105 of the Evidence Act placed the unmet burden squarely on the accused. The precedents pressed by the defence — including Khima Vikamshi — were distinguished on their facts.</p>

<h2>Investigation flaws and their limits</h2>
<p>The defence pointed to the usual gaps: clothes of the witnesses not seized, neighbours not examined. The court acknowledged the first as a possible flaw while holding it "not fatal" — a formulation consistent with the settled position that the prosecution case is tested on the evidence actually led, and lapses matter only when they create real doubt about that evidence. Where two consistent eyewitnesses are corroborated by medicine and recovery, the absence of a forensic garnish does not acquit.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> A private defence strategy must be chosen, not hedged. It concedes presence and the act, and stakes everything on apprehension and proportionality — which means the defence record must be built for it: injuries on the accused proved through the MLC, cross-examination establishing who was armed, and a Section 313 narrative consistent with the plea. Running it alongside a denial of presence, as here, hands the prosecution the phrase every judgment then repeats: the pleas are mutually destructive.</p>
</div>
